 ## Overview
-Chunked Ray Rendering is a memory-management strategy for object-order volume rendering that processes large batches of rays in smaller, fixed-size chunks to prevent Out-Of-Memory (OOM) errors on the GPU [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. It is primarily used during validation and inference phases where full-resolution images must be generated without the training-time optimizations like CUDA-accelerated raymarching [S3, S4, unofficial]. It builds on [Batched Ray Rendering](1547-batched-ray-rendering.md) by explicitly managing the batch size relative to available VRAM, and differs from [Chunked Trilinear Volume Rendering](2557-chunked-trilinear-volume-rendering.md) by chunking the *rays* (the query set) rather than the *volume data* (the lookup grid) [S2, S5, unofficial].
+Chunked Ray Rendering is a memory-management strategy for object-order volume rendering that processes large batches of rays in smaller, fixed-size chunks to prevent Out-Of-Memory (OOM) errors on the GPU [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. It is primarily used during validation and inference phases where full-resolution images must be generated without training-time optimizations like CUDA-accelerated raymarching [S3, S4, unofficial]. It builds on [Batched Ray Rendering](1547-batched-ray-rendering.md) by explicitly managing the batch size relative to available VRAM, and differs from [Chunked Trilinear Volume Rendering](2557-chunked-trilinear-volume-rendering.md) by chunking the *rays* (the query set) rather than the *volume data* (the lookup grid) [S2, S5, unofficial].
 
